#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Verify registry/skills.yml against the producer bodies it claims (.github#247, ADR-0017).

`skills.yml` declares, per row, a `source:` (the owning repo's canonical SKILL.md) and the
`sha256:` it was reconciled from. Its header says the rows are "GENERATED / RECONCILED FROM THE
PRODUCER MANIFESTS -- never hand-authored bytes", and every changelog entry restates the invariant
**registry = manifest = bytes**.

Nothing enforced it. Each producer repo drift-guards its OWN manifest against its OWN bodies
(FS.GG.SDD `registry skill-manifest --check`, FS.GG.Rendering / FS.GG.Game
`generate-skill-manifest.fsx --check`), but no check tied a registry row to the remote body it
names. A producer could merge a body change and this registry silently went stale -- which is
exactly what happened to `fs-gg-audio` (ADR-0024 step 4), to `fs-gg-game-core` and
`fs-gg-model-swap` before it (registry changelog 2026-07-06), and to 14 of 32 rows by the time
this check was written.

Checks, all over local checkouts (no network -- CI checks out the producer repos).

Over each row the registry ALREADY declares:

  1. SOURCE EXISTS   every row's `source:` resolves to a file in its owning repo. A renamed or
                     relocated skill currently goes unnoticed.
  2. DIGEST MATCHES  sha256(canonical body) == the declared `sha256:`.
Over the SET of rows -- the converse direction, and the reason this file grew (.github#289, epic
#266 "coherence gates that fail open"). Checks 1-2 are each a predicate over a row that is already
declared, so a shipped skill simply ABSENT from the catalog was not a finding, it was nothing. Four
of them were: `fs-gg-collision`, `fs-gg-grids`, `fs-gg-line-drawing`, `fs-gg-visibility` -- real,
digest-matching, emitted by `--profile game`, and invisible to the gate guarding the registry
ADR-0017 calls the "single authoritative catalog". This is ADR-0014's presence-vs-content lesson
recurring in the opposite direction: we validated the CONTENT OF WHAT IS LISTED and never the
COMPLETENESS OF THE LIST.

  4. MANIFEST FOUND  every producer named by a `source:` prefix has a readable, well-shaped
                     manifest. A manifest that vanished would silently drop every skill it
                     declares out of check 5 -- the same fail-open, one level up. Fails closed.
  5. DECLARED COMPLETENESS
                     every skill any producer manifest declares has a row here. Matched on `id`
                     alone (the registry's key), so Rendering's frozen re-declaration of a
                     Game-owned skill is satisfied by the `fs-gg-game` row.

                     AND, SINCE .github#2547, THE SAME QUESTION ASKED OF THE POPULATION: every
                     FS-GG repository `registry/repos.yml` rosters must be VISIBLE under
                     `--repos-root`. Check 5 above is a predicate over the producers this run
                     happened to find, so a producer nobody checked out was not a finding, it was
                     nothing — which is #289's own lesson ("we validated the CONTENT OF WHAT IS
                     LISTED and never the COMPLETENESS OF THE LIST") recurring one level up, over
                     the list of PRODUCERS. FS.GG.Templates shipped a manifest declaring six
                     `scope: product` skills; the CI clone loop was a hardcoded
                     `for repo in FS.GG.SDD FS.GG.Rendering FS.GG.Game`, so its manifest was
                     reconciled against nothing and its six absent rows were unreportable. Absence
                     is the finding, not "no manifest here": the gate cannot tell a non-producer
                     from an unreachable producer without looking. A rostered repo that IS present
                     and carries no manifest stays silently fine.

  8. PARAMETER VOCABULARY
                     every parameter a row's `materializes-when` names is in the registry's
                     `parameters:` list. That list declared the ONE materialization vocabulary the
                     catalog models and nothing read it, so the catalog could assert
                     `[profile, lifecycle, feedback, designSystem]` org-wide while a row evaluated
                     `template` (.github#2547). NECESSARY, NOT SUFFICIENT, and the distinction is
                     load-bearing: nothing evaluates a predicate against this list —
                     `skill-union-assert.sh --params` reads a scaffold's `scaffold-provenance.json
                     -> effectiveParameters` — so a parameter declared here but absent from that
                     provenance still answers `false` for every scaffold its producer builds.
                     Declaring it makes the catalog honest about the axes it carries; it does not
                     make a predicate answerable.

And over the CONTENT of a row the manifest also declares (.github#292, epic #266). Checks 1-3 are
predicates over a row's BYTES; check 5 over its PRESENCE. Nothing compared the one field that is
neither -- the emission condition:

  6. PREDICATE MATCHES
                     a row's `materializes-when` equals the declaring manifest's. This is what
                     `skill-union-assert.sh --params` evaluates to decide whether a skill's ABSENCE
                     from a scaffold is legitimate (ADR-0017's `[missing]`/`[unexpected]` classes),
                     so a stale predicate makes the union gate wrong in a direction it cannot
                     detect -- and the digests stay green throughout. It hid `fs-gg-testing` at
                     `profile == governed` for six days after Rendering deliberately widened it to
                     all five profiles (Rendering#90, 2026-07-04), which is exactly the scaffold
                     the registry then claimed should NOT carry a testing skill.

                     Compared on a WHITESPACE-NORMALIZED form (list spacing is not semantics), never
                     on grammar: `profile in [a,b]` == `profile in [a, b]`. It deliberately does NOT
                     normalize the C-style grammar (`(profile == "app" || ...)`) Rendering shipped
                     before Rendering#77. Absorbing that here would make a producer's regression to a
                     grammar the ADR-0017 evaluator CANNOT PARSE -- and therefore reads as false --
                     compare equal and report green: the fails-open shape this epic exists to close.
                     A grammar regression must surface as a divergence. Whether the gate should also
                     reject non-canonical grammar outright is .github#290, not this check.

                     The row's `owner:` picks which manifest is authoritative. If two declarers
                     disagree with EACH OTHER, the row is reported and `--write` refuses to guess.

  7. CROSS-REFERENCES
                     a materialized skill's own text must not depend on a SIBLING the same
                     materialization can legitimately drop (.github#2366). `work-roadmap`/
                     `work-board` (`materializes-when: always`) instruct a worker to run
                     `.agents/skills/fs-gg-feedback-report/scripts/feedback-tool.fsx` — a path that
                     belongs to a DIFFERENT row. Checks 1-6 are each a predicate over ONE row (its
                     bytes, its presence, its own predicate against its own manifest); nothing
                     compared TWO rows' predicates to EACH OTHER, so two individually-coherent rows
                     could still combine unsafely with nothing here to say so. For every row whose
                     own `materializes-when` is satisfiable (an unreachable predicate, e.g. an
                     operator skill's `"false"`, can never surface this failure mode and is
                     skipped), this scans its `SKILL.md` and every file under its `references/` for
                     a path-shaped reference to a sibling id and asserts the referencing row's
                     predicate IMPLIES the referenced row's — checked by enumeration over the
                     domain of parameter values the registry's OWN `materializes-when` corpus
                     already uses (a scaffold's actual, but registry-external, effectiveParameters
                     are `skill-union-assert.sh --params`'s business, not this check's; see that
                     script's `eval_clause`/`eval_and`/`eval_condition`, whose grammar this mirrors
                     rather than re-inventing). A referenced id absent from the registry entirely is
                     the same failure by construction (an unregistered target is trivially never
                     guaranteed present). Registry-internal and requires no live scaffolded product
                     tree — this is exactly what lets `.github`'s own CI catch the class of drift
                     before it ever reaches one.

ADR-0022 §6's frozen-mirror field and byte-comparison family were retired at P6 by .github#1862
after Rendering removed the second copies (Rendering#1147). This checker now answers only catalog,
digest, and materialization-predicate questions. It deliberately ignores legacy producer-manifest
fields until each producer removes them; it does not preserve another mirror classification.

VERSIONED PACKAGE DELIVERY DOES NOT MAKE THIS DIGEST REDUNDANT. A package version proves that
receivers installing that version receive its contents; it cannot prove that this central catalog's
snapshot still names the current producer body. The producer's own drift guard detects a manifest
that disagrees with its body. This check's distinct `digest-matches` finding detects the remaining
class: a producer and its manifest have advanced consistently while `registry/skills.yml` remains at
the earlier digest. That is catalog freshness, not a second receiver-byte comparison. The #1861
measurement exercised it by mutating one declared digest against fresh producer checkouts: one
`digest-matches` finding resulted, and `--write` restored the exact current snapshot.

Producers are DERIVED, never hardcoded: the repos named by `source:` prefixes (which MUST carry a
manifest), plus any other checkout under `--repos-root` that carries one -- so a brand-new producer
whose rows are ALL missing is still read, rather than being invisible for want of a row to name it.

THAT DERIVATION IS ONLY AS COMPLETE AS `--repos-root`, WHICH IS WHY THE ROSTER ARM EXISTS. Both
sentences above answer "who did we find?"; neither answered "who should we have been able to look
at?", and the de facto answer was a hardcoded `for repo in ...` in skill-registry-coherence.yml
plus, in skill-registry-autofix.yml, the `source:` prefixes OF THE REGISTRY BEING RECONCILED -- a
loop that is self-referential in the one direction that matters, since a producer with no rows yet
is never cloned and so its rows can never be appended. `--producers` now prints the roster
`registry/repos.yml` declares and BOTH loops read it, so the expected set has exactly one spelling
and `roster_reachable` reds when the realised set is smaller (.github#2547).

KNOWN BOUND, stated rather than hidden: a producer is recognised by a manifest at one of
MANIFEST_CANDIDATES, directly under its checkout. A repo that publishes its manifest somewhere else
is not seen at all -- and, if the registry names no row from it, is not seen by check 4 either. CI
checks the producers out at exactly these paths, so this is a shape the gate requires, not a hole it
tolerates; a producer that moves its manifest must add the new location here. That obligation is not
theoretical: FS.GG.SDD moved its process manifest from `.agents/skills/` to `.claude/skills/` because
ADR-0067 §6 makes the former a generated view that a bare clone does not have, and this list gained
the tracked root in the same landing (#1757, FS-GG/FS.GG.SDD#771). `.github` is itself a
producer of its own authored DRIVER skills (ADR-0054); it emits its manifest at the third candidate
(`registry/driver-skill-manifest.json`) and is reached not by a network clone but by its own
checkout, which skill-registry-coherence.yml makes visible under `--repos-root` as `.github`.

Two producers may declare the SAME id. When such an id is MISSING from the registry, which repo owns
it is not derivable from the manifests, so the row is reported and `--write` refuses to guess an
`owner:`/`source:` for it.

Digest semantics match `scripts/repos.sh digest`, `Fsgg.SkillMirror.sha256`, and both producer
generators: lowercase hex over the UTF-8 bytes of the body TEXT, with a leading BOM stripped so a
BOM can never enter the digest on either the producing or the verifying side.

Pure-stdlib + PyYAML (already a coherence-gate dependency); no network, no tokens.

Usage:
  scripts/fsgg-skill-registry-check                       # check (default); exit 1 on any finding
  scripts/fsgg-skill-registry-check --write               # reconcile in place, exit 0 if clean
  scripts/fsgg-skill-registry-check --json                # machine findings
  scripts/fsgg-skill-registry-check --repos-root DIR      # where the producer checkouts live
  scripts/fsgg-skill-registry-check --registry PATH       # explicit skills.yml

Exit: 0 = coherent (or --write left it coherent); 1 = findings / usage error.

`--write` rewrites ONLY the `sha256:` and `materializes-when:` values of a stale row and adds a
row for a skill a manifest declares that the registry lacks, in place, leaving byte-for-byte
everything else (the file is hand-formatted, aligned YAML flow maps -- a full yaml.dump would
destroy it). Both rewrites take the value FROM the manifest -- the digest verbatim, the predicate in
the whitespace-normalized form it is compared in -- which is what "reconciled from the producer
manifests" means; neither invents one. A NEW row is
reconciled FROM the manifest entry: `scope`/`sha256`/`materializes-when` verbatim, `source` from the
entry's `supplied-by`, `owner` from the producer repo. A manifest that omits `supplied-by` (SDD's
process manifest does) gives no `source:` to derive, so the row is REPORTED and not added --
inventing a path would be hand-authoring the bytes the registry header forbids.

A new row is HOMED, not tail-appended, whenever the file already holds its siblings -- an existing
row of the SAME `owner:` and the SAME normalized `materializes-when:`. It is inserted immediately
after the last such sibling, with a generated annotation, so it lands in the block it belongs to
rather than in a tail the operator has to re-home by hand (the .github#1198 toil; .github#299/#1200).
A row with NO such sibling cannot be confidently homed -- the tool does not GUESS a block -- so it
falls back to the tail append under the existing "re-home and annotate these" note.

With `--now DATE`, `--write` also does the bookkeeping the registry protocol used to leave to the
operator: it bumps `updated:` to DATE and prepends one dated `registry/skills.CHANGELOG.md` entry
naming what it reconciled. The date is INJECTED, never read from a clock, so a reconcile stays
deterministic (the same seam `skill-registry-autofix-body --now` uses). Without `--now` the value
bytes are still reconciled exactly as before and the two housekeeping steps are left to the operator,
with the reminder printed -- so the flag is additive and every existing caller is unchanged.

Whatever it wrote, `--write` then RE-CHECKS from disk and exits non-zero on anything left, so it can
never report success over a registry that is still incoherent.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import hashlib
import itertools
import json
import os
import re
import sys

# PyYAML is imported lazily by `check()` (see `_load_registry`), NOT at module load: the
# reference seams (`--normalize-when`, and importing `normalize_when` for the cross-impl conformance
# fixture, .github#398) are pure-stdlib and must run on a runner that never installed PyYAML — the
# skill-union selftest is one. Reading a registry still hard-requires it, with the same message.

# THE CHECKS THAT MEAN "I COULD NOT LOOK" — not "I looked and it is broken".
#
# A `manifest-found` finding is a producer manifest this tool could not read or parse. It is NOT a
# judgement case, however much it looks like one: it means the rows from that producer were never
# examined at all, so EVERY verdict derived from that run is unsound — including a comparison that
# concludes nothing changed. `skill-registry-retire-gate` already refuses to reason over one for
# exactly this reason (#425): *"the resulting empty diff is not evidence of ANYTHING."*
#
# It lives HERE, in the tool that EMITS the finding, and the retire gate imports it — the same rule
# that file already applies to `classify`: re-encoding the set somewhere else is how "which findings
# blind us?" ends up computed in three places that agree in none (#485). One definition, imported.
BLINDING_CHECKS = {"manifest-found"}

# Where a producer publishes its `skill-manifest` (schema v1). SDD EMITS its process manifest into
# its TRACKED SOURCE agent-skill root; Rendering and Game publish theirs from the template tree;
# `.github` emits its DRIVER manifest into the registry dir it owns (ADR-0054 — `.github` authors its
# own product-materialized `scope: driver` skills, e.g. work-roadmap). Probed in order; the first hit
# wins. Locations, not repo names -- the producer SET is derived (see producers()).
#
# `.claude/skills/` LEADS, AND `.agents/skills/` IS THE ONE IT REPLACES (#1757, FS-GG/FS.GG.SDD#771).
# ADR-0067 §6 turns `.agents/skills` into a GENERATED VIEW: untracked, git-ignored, and absent in a
# bare checkout by construction — which is what `skill-registry-coherence.yml` makes of every named
# producer (`git clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none`, no view generation). A producer whose manifest
# lived only there therefore had NO manifest here at all, and `find_manifest` would return None,
# firing the fail-closed `manifest-found` finding and taking all 16 of SDD's process rows out of
# digest coverage. Adding the tracked root is what keeps that from happening; keeping the view root
# is what keeps a producer that has not moved yet working, and costs nothing.
#
# THE ORDER IS THE ANSWER TO "WHICH ONE WINS", AND IT IS NOT ARBITRARY. A transitional tree can carry
# BOTH — during a move, or simply because a LINK-mode `skill-view generate` makes the view root
# resolve to the source. The tracked source is the authority in every one of those cases: it is what
# git carries, what a reviewer reads, and what the producer's own writer emits. Reading the view
# first would mean grading a repo against a file whose existence depends on whether a checkout step
# happened to run — the shape ADR-0067 §8 exists to remove. So the tracked root leads.
MANIFEST_CANDIDATES = (
    ".claude/skills/skill-manifest.json",
    ".agents/skills/skill-manifest.json",
    "template/skill-manifest/skill-manifest.json",
    "registry/driver-skill-manifest.json",
)

# ADR-0017: an entry with no `materializes-when` is unconditionally emitted.
DEFAULT_WHEN = "always"

# A canonical-body digest: 64 lowercase hex chars. Anything else is a registry-shape defect.
DIGEST_RE = re.compile(r"[0-9a-f]{64}")


def canonical_digest(raw: bytes) -> str:
    """sha256 over the body text's UTF-8 bytes, BOM-free and CRLF-folded (Fsgg.SkillMirror.sha256).

    TWO NORMALIZATIONS, BOTH THE LIBRARY'S (.github#1547). The BOM strip mirrors the library's
    callers, which decode the file as UTF-8 and whose decoder consumes the BOM. The CRLF fold
    mirrors `Fsgg.SkillMirror.sha256` itself, which does `body.Replace("\\r\\n", "\\n")` before
    hashing (feature 070) so an LF-authored body does not spuriously drift on a CRLF checkout.

    WHY THE FOLD IS HERE NOW. The canonical digest had THREE implementations — this one,
    `skill_digest` in scripts/skill-union-assert.sh, and the library — and only the library folded
    CRLF, so on a Windows/`eol=crlf` checkout the two shells reported a drift the library did not.
    ADR-0014 names `Fsgg.SkillMirror` the ONE implementation and .github#120 records that this
    repo's checkers FOLLOW it, so the two shells were the drifted copies; the 2026-07-27 decision on
    #1547 aligned both. CONSEQUENCE, STATED PLAINLY: a CRLF file and its LF twin now have the SAME
    canonical digest, so this is deliberately slightly more permissive than a byte comparison.

    IT FOLDS THE PAIR, NOT THE CHARACTER. Deleting every ``\\r`` would also drop LONE carriage
    returns, which the library keeps — and that collapses ``b"x\\r"`` and ``b"x"`` onto ONE digest,
    two distinct bodies sharing a value, which is a fail-open (#266) rather than a cosmetic
    difference. Done on BYTES rather than decoded text on purpose: CR and LF cannot occur inside a
    multi-byte UTF-8 sequence, so the byte-wise replace is exactly the library's string-wise one
    without this checker having to decode (and therefore without it having to decide what to do with
    input that is not valid UTF-8).
    """
    if raw.startswith(b"\xef\xbb\xbf"):
        raw = raw[3:]
    return hashlib.sha256(raw.replace(b"\r\n", b"\n")).hexdigest()


def read_bytes(path: str) -> bytes | None:
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as handle:
            return handle.read()
    except OSError:
        return None


def owner_of(repo: str) -> str:
    """The registry's `owner:` slug for a producer checkout: FS.GG.Rendering -> fs-gg-rendering.

    `.github` is the sole producer whose slug is NOT the dot->dash of its repo name (ADR-0054): it
    authors its own driver skills and its owner slug IS `.github`. Without this the dot->dash rule
    yields `-github`, which matches no row's `owner:` (predicates() would fall through to "all
    declarers") and would be synthesized onto a `--write`-appended row (completeness())."""
    if repo == ".github":
        return ".github"
    return repo.lower().replace(".", "-")


def find_manifest(repos_root: str, repo: str) -> str | None:
    for candidate in MANIFEST_CANDIDATES:
        path = os.path.join(repos_root, repo, candidate)
        if os.path.isfile(path):
            return path
    return None


def producers(repos_root: str, rows: list[dict]) -> tuple[list[str], list[str]]:
    """(repos that MUST carry a manifest, other checkouts that happen to carry one).

    The first set is derived from the `source:` prefixes -- those repos are the ones the registry
    claims to have been reconciled from, so a manifest missing there is a finding, not a skip.
    The second catches a producer the registry names NOWHERE (every one of its rows missing) --
    which the first set, being keyed on existing rows, is structurally unable to see.
    """
    named = sorted({row["source"].split("/")[0] for row in rows if isinstance(row.get("source"), str)})
    try:
        present = sorted(d for d in os.listdir(repos_root) if os.path.isdir(os.path.join(repos_root, d)))
    except OSError:
        present = []
    unnamed = [r for r in present if r not in named and find_manifest(repos_root, r)]
    return named, unnamed


def roster_path_for(registry_path: str) -> str:
    """`registry/repos.yml` beside the `registry/skills.yml` this run judges.

    A SIBLING rather than a flag with a default, because the two files are one directory by
    construction and a flag is a thing a caller can quietly stop passing — which is the exact
    failure this roster check exists to close, reintroduced one level up.
    """
    return os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(registry_path)), "repos.yml")


def roster(registry_path: str) -> tuple[list[str], str]:
    """(the FS-GG checkout names `registry/repos.yml` rosters, error) — the set that MUST be visible.

    DERIVED, NOT RESTATED (.github#2547). `producers()` above answers "who did we find?", over
    whatever happens to be under `--repos-root`. Nothing answered "who should we have been able to
    look at?", so the honest answer was whatever the CI clone loop happened to list — and that loop
    was a hardcoded `for repo in FS.GG.SDD FS.GG.Rendering FS.GG.Game` in
    skill-registry-coherence.yml (and, in skill-registry-autofix.yml, the `source:` prefixes of the
    registry BEING RECONCILED, which is self-referential: a producer with no rows yet is never
    cloned, so its rows can never be appended).

    FS.GG.Templates shipped a producer manifest declaring six `scope: product` skills and was in
    neither list. Every arm below then ran, green, over a set that silently excluded it: its
    manifest was reconciled against nothing and its absence from the catalog was not a finding, it
    was nothing. That is #289's fail-open — "we validated the CONTENT OF WHAT IS LISTED and never
    the COMPLETENESS OF THE LIST" — recurring one level up, over the list of PRODUCERS rather than
    the list of skills.

    The roster this reads is `registry/repos.yml`, the organisation's own repository roster, which
    has carried `FS-GG/FS.GG.Templates` since long before it became a skill producer. So the check
    fires on the real cause the day it lands, rather than only on producers somebody remembered to
    add to a second list here. Non-FS-GG rows (`EHotwagner/S.I.R.`, `role: non-participant`) are
    excluded: this organisation does not clone them and does not catalog their skills.
    """
    path = roster_path_for(registry_path)
    try:
        import yaml
    except ImportError:  # pragma: no cover - environment guard
        return [], "PyYAML is required to read the repository roster"
    try:
        with open(path) as handle:
            doc = yaml.safe_load(handle)
    except (OSError, ValueError) as exc:
        return [], f"unreadable repository roster {path}: {exc}"
    rows = doc.get("repos") if isinstance(doc, dict) else None
    if not isinstance(rows, list):
        return [], f"repository roster has no `repos` list: {path}"
    names = []
    for row in rows:
        full = row.get("full") if isinstance(row, dict) else None
        if isinstance(full, str) and full.startswith("FS-GG/"):
            names.append(full.split("/", 1)[1])
    return sorted(set(names)), ""


def roster_reachable(registry_path: str, repos_root: str) -> list[dict]:
    """Every rostered FS-GG repository must be VISIBLE under `--repos-root` (.github#2547).

    Reported under the `declared-completeness` arm, because it is the same question that arm has
    always asked, asked of the population instead of the members: a repository this gate cannot see
    is a repository whose producer manifest — if it has one — is reconciled against nothing.

    ABSENCE IS THE FINDING, NOT "no manifest here". The gate cannot tell a non-producer from an
    unreachable producer without looking, and answering the question by not asking it is precisely
    how six shipped product skills stayed invisible. A rostered repo that IS present and carries no
    manifest is silently fine (FS.GG.Governance, FS.GG.Audio and FS.GG.Net are all in that state
    today) — `producers()` already skips it. Only the ones nobody checked out are reported.
    """
    names, error = roster(registry_path)
    if error:
        return [
            {
                "id": "registry/repos.yml",
                "check": "declared-completeness",
                "detail": (
                    f"{error} — the set of repositories whose producer manifests this gate must be "
                    "able to see is unknown, so every arm below would run over whatever happened to "
                    "be checked out"
                ),
            }
        ]
    findings = []
    for repo in names:
        if os.path.isdir(os.path.join(repos_root, repo)):
            continue
        findings.append(
            {
                "id": repo,
                "check": "declared-completeness",
                "detail": (
                    f"registry/repos.yml rosters {repo} but it is absent from --repos-root "
                    f"({repos_root}) — if it carries a producer skill-manifest, that manifest is "
                    "reconciled against NOTHING here and every skill it declares is invisible to "
                    "this gate (the .github#2547 cause); check it out, or drop it from the roster"
                ),
            }
        )
    return findings


def parameter_vocabulary(registry: dict) -> list[dict]:
    """Every parameter a `materializes-when` names must be in the registry's `parameters:` list.

    The list is the catalog's declaration of the ONE materialization vocabulary it models, and it
    was decoration: nothing read it. `registry/skills.yml` declared
    `[profile, lifecycle, feedback, designSystem]` while FS.GG.Templates' six product-skill
    predicates are written over `template`, and no gate could say the two disagreed (.github#2547).

    THIS IS NECESSARY AND NOT SUFFICIENT, AND SAYING SO IS THE POINT. Nothing evaluates a predicate
    against this list — `scripts/skill-union-assert.sh --params` reads a scaffold's
    `scaffold-provenance.json -> effectiveParameters`, and a parameter that is declared here but
    absent from that provenance still answers `false` for every scaffold. Declaring it makes the
    catalog HONEST about the axes it carries; it does not make a predicate ANSWERABLE. The second
    half is the producer's, and for `template` it is tracked separately — see the `template` entry
    in the registry's own `parameters:` comment.
    """
    findings: list[dict] = []
    declared = registry.get("parameters")
    used: dict[str, list[str]] = {}
    for row in registry.get("skills", []):
        when = str(row.get("materializes-when", DEFAULT_WHEN))
        for name in sorted(_relevant_keys(when)):
            used.setdefault(name, []).append(str(row.get("id")))
    if not isinstance(declared, list):
        if not used:
            return findings
        return [
            {
                "id": "parameters",
                "check": "parameter-vocabulary",
                "detail": (
                    f"the registry declares predicates over {', '.join(sorted(used))} but carries no "
                    "`parameters:` list — the vocabulary its predicates are written in is undeclared"
                ),
            }
        ]
    declared_set = {str(p) for p in declared}
    for name in sorted(used):
        if name in declared_set:
            continue
        rows = ", ".join(sorted(set(used[name]))[:6])
        findings.append(
            {
                "id": name,
                "check": "parameter-vocabulary",
                "detail": (
                    f"`{name}` is referenced by the `materializes-when` of {rows} but is not in the "
                    f"registry's `parameters:` list ({', '.join(sorted(declared_set))}) — the catalog "
                    "asserts one materialization vocabulary org-wide while a row evaluates a "
                    "different one"
                ),
            }
        )
    return findings


def read_manifest(path: str) -> tuple[list[dict] | None, str]:
    """(entries, error). A manifest we cannot read or make sense of is a finding, never a skip."""
    try:
        with open(path) as handle:
            doc = json.load(handle)
    except (OSError, ValueError) as exc:
        return None, f"unreadable manifest {path}: {exc}"
    entries = doc.get("skills") if isinstance(doc, dict) else None
    if not isinstance(entries, list):
        return None, f"manifest has no `skills` array: {path}"
    # Guard the ELEMENTS too, not just the array: `{"skills": ["fs-gg-oops"]}` would otherwise
    # reach entry.get() and raise. A manifest we cannot make sense of is a finding, never a crash.
    bad = [i for i, entry in enumerate(entries) if not isinstance(entry, dict)]
    if bad:
        return None, f"manifest `skills[{bad[0]}]` is not an object: {path}"
    return entries, ""


def normalize_when(value: object) -> str:
    """A `materializes-when` in comparable form: whitespace collapsed, list spacing regular.

    Whitespace is not semantics — `profile in [a,b]` and `profile in [a, b]` are the same predicate,
    and a producer reformatting its manifest must not read as drift. GRAMMAR is semantics, and is
    deliberately left alone: see check 6 in the module docstring.

    A double-quoted STRING LITERAL is bytes, not layout: `name == "a,b"` must not become
    `name == "a, b"`. `--write` persists this exact text into the registry, where the union gate
    parses it, so normalizing inside a literal would rewrite the value the gate compares against.
    Literals are stashed behind NUL-delimited placeholders (which carry no space, comma or bracket
    of their own) and restored afterwards.
    """
    literals: list[str] = []

    def stash(match: re.Match) -> str:
        literals.append(match.group(0))
        return f"\x00{len(literals) - 1}\x00"

    text = re.sub(r'"(?:[^"\\]|\\.)*"', stash, str(value))
    text = " ".join(text.split())
    text = re.sub(r"\s*,\s*", ", ", text)
    text = re.sub(r"\[\s*", "[", text)
    text = re.sub(r"\s*\]", "]", text)
    return re.sub(r"\x00(\d+)\x00", lambda m: literals[int(m.group(1))], text)


def when_of(entry: dict) -> str:
    """A manifest entry's predicate, normalized. Absent ⇒ `always` (ADR-0017)."""
    return normalize_when(entry.get("materializes-when", DEFAULT_WHEN))


def manifest_index(repos_root: str, rows: list[dict]) -> tuple[dict[str, list[dict]], list[dict]]:
    """({skill_id: [{repo, entry}, ...]}, manifest-found findings) over every producer.

    Read ONCE and shared by checks 5 and 6: `declared-completeness` wants the ids this index has
    that the registry lacks, `predicate-matches` the ids they have in common. Records EVERY
    declarer of an id — taking the first would attribute by repo sort order when multiple producer
    manifests legitimately share an id.
    """
    named, unnamed = producers(repos_root, rows)

    findings: list[dict] = []
    declarers: dict[str, list[dict]] = {}

    for repo in named + unnamed:
        manifest_path = find_manifest(repos_root, repo)
        if manifest_path is None:
            # Only reachable for a `named` repo: `unnamed` was filtered on having one.
            findings.append(
                {
                    "id": repo,
                    "check": "manifest-found",
                    "detail": (
                        f"{repo} is named by a `source:` prefix but carries no skill-manifest "
                        f"(looked for {', '.join(MANIFEST_CANDIDATES)}) — every skill it declares "
                        "would go unchecked"
                    ),
                }
            )
            continue

        entries, error = read_manifest(manifest_path)
        if entries is None:
            findings.append({"id": repo, "check": "manifest-found", "detail": error})
            continue

        for entry in entries:
            skill_id = entry.get("id")
            if not skill_id:
                continue
            declarers.setdefault(skill_id, []).append({"repo": repo, "entry": entry})

    return declarers, findings


def predicates(rows: list[dict], declarers: dict[str, list[dict]]) -> list[dict]:
    """A row's `materializes-when` must equal its declaring manifest's (.github#292).

    A row NO manifest declares is not this check's business: either its producer's manifest is
    unreadable (check 4 already failed closed on it) or the row names a producer that declares
    nothing — both louder findings than a predicate mismatch would be.
    """
    findings: list[dict] = []

    for row in rows:
        skill_id = row["id"]
        declared_by = declarers.get(skill_id)
        if not declared_by:
            continue

        # The frozen mirror re-declares an id it does not own; `owner:` says whose word is law.
        owned = [d for d in declared_by if owner_of(d["repo"]) == row.get("owner")]
        authoritative = owned or declared_by
        manifest_whens = {when_of(d["entry"]) for d in authoritative}

        if len(manifest_whens) > 1:
            repos = ", ".join(sorted(d["repo"] for d in authoritative))
            findings.append(
                {
                    "id": skill_id,
                    "check": "predicate-matches",
                    "detail": (
                        f"its {len(authoritative)} declaring manifests ({repos}) disagree on "
                        f"`materializes-when` ({' vs '.join(sorted(repr(w) for w in manifest_whens))}) "
                        "— --write cannot reconcile a row against producers that contradict each "
                        "other; fix the producers first"
                    ),
                }
            )
            continue

        manifest_when = next(iter(manifest_whens))
        row_when = normalize_when(row.get("materializes-when", DEFAULT_WHEN))
        if row_when == manifest_when:
            continue

        repo = authoritative[0]["repo"]
        finding = {
            "id": skill_id,
            "check": "predicate-matches",
            "detail": (
                f"registry says `{row_when}` but {repo}'s manifest declares "
                f"`{manifest_when}` — the union gate evaluates this to decide whether the "
                "skill's absence from a scaffold is legitimate"
            ),
            "declared": row_when,
        }
        # `--write` rewrites a VALUE in place; a row that omits the field has none to rewrite, and
        # inserting one would re-align the hand-formatted flow map. Report it instead — `actual` is
        # what marks a finding reconcilable (an absent field reads as `always`, ADR-0017, so this is
        # only reachable when the manifest declares something else).
        if "materializes-when" in row:
            finding["actual"] = manifest_when
        else:
            finding["detail"] += (
                "; --write cannot rewrite it (the row declares no `materializes-when:` field) "
                "— add it by hand"
            )
        findings.append(finding)

    return findings


def completeness(rows: list[dict], declarers: dict[str, list[dict]]) -> list[dict]:
    """Every skill a producer manifest declares must have a row here (.github#289)."""
    declared = {row["id"] for row in rows}

    findings: list[dict] = []
    missing = {i: d for i, d in declarers.items() if i not in declared}

    for skill_id, declared_by in sorted(missing.items()):
        if len(declared_by) > 1:
            repos = ", ".join(d["repo"] for d in declared_by)
            findings.append(
                {
                    "id": skill_id,
                    "check": "declared-completeness",
                    "detail": (
                        f"declared by {len(declared_by)} producer manifests ({repos}) but absent from "
                        "the registry; --write cannot append it (which repo is the owner and which "
                        "the authoritative owner is not derivable from the manifests) — add the row by hand"
                    ),
                }
            )
            continue

        repo, entry = declared_by[0]["repo"], declared_by[0]["entry"]
        supplied_by = entry.get("supplied-by")
        when = str(entry.get("materializes-when", DEFAULT_WHEN))
        finding = {
            "id": skill_id,
            "check": "declared-completeness",
            "detail": (
                f"declared by {repo}'s manifest but absent from the registry "
                f"(materializes-when: {when})"
            ),
        }
        # `--write` can only append a row whose `source:` the manifest actually gives us.
        digest = str(entry.get("sha256", ""))
        if supplied_by and DIGEST_RE.fullmatch(digest):
            finding["row"] = {
                "id": skill_id,
                "scope": str(entry.get("scope", "product")),
                "owner": owner_of(repo),
                "source": f"{repo}/{supplied_by.rstrip('/')}/SKILL.md",
                "sha256": digest,
                "materializes-when": when,
            }
        else:
            why = "no `supplied-by`" if not supplied_by else f"unusable sha256 {digest!r}"
            finding["detail"] += f"; --write cannot append it ({why}) — add the row by hand"
        findings.append(finding)

    return findings


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CHECK 7: CROSS-REFERENCES (.github#2366). See the module docstring's "CROSS-REFERENCES" section
# for the finding this proves and why it is registry-internal. The grammar below is a deliberate,
# minimal PORT of `scripts/skill-union-assert.sh`'s `eval_clause`/`eval_and`/`eval_condition`
# (lines ~304-355 there) — same tokens, same precedence — so a predicate reads the same way in
# both checkers rather than this file inventing a second grammar to drift against the first.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

_WHEN_IN_RE = re.compile(r"([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\s+in\s+\[([^\]]*)\]")
_WHEN_CMP_RE = re.compile(r'([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\s*(?:==|!=)\s*("(?:[^"\\]|\\.)*"|[^\s]+)')

# A path-shaped reference to ANOTHER skill's directory, e.g. `.agents/skills/fs-gg-feedback-report/
# scripts/feedback-tool.fsx`. Deliberately narrow (AC-001's own wording: "a sibling skill path"),
# not a bare-name/backtick mention — a doc that merely NAMES another skill in prose is not a
# dependency on that skill's presence the way a path a worker is told to RUN is.
_SIBLING_PATH_RE = re.compile(r"\.(?:agents|claude)/skills/([a-z][a-z0-9-]*)/")


def _unquote_when(token: str) -> str:
    token = token.strip()
    if len(token) >= 2 and token[0] == '"' and token[-1] == '"':
        return token[1:-1]
    return token


def eval_clause(clause: str, params: dict[str, str]) -> bool:
    """One comparison clause. Mirrors `skill-union-assert.sh`'s `eval_clause`."""
    text = clause.strip()
    if text in ("always", "true"):
        return True
    if text == "false":
        return False
    if " in [" in text:
        key, _, rest = text.partition(" in [")
        rhs = rest[:-1] if rest.endswith("]") else rest
        for item in rhs.split(","):
            item_t = _unquote_when(item)
            if item_t and params.get(key.strip(), "") == item_t:
                return True
        return False
    if "!=" in text:
        key, _, rhs = text.partition("!=")
        return params.get(key.strip(), "") != _unquote_when(rhs)
    if "==" in text:
        key, _, rhs = text.partition("==")
        return params.get(key.strip(), "") == _unquote_when(rhs)
    raise ValueError(f"unparseable materializes-when clause: {clause!r}")


def eval_and(conjunction: str, params: dict[str, str]) -> bool:
    """Clauses joined by ` and ` — all must hold. Mirrors `skill-union-assert.sh`'s `eval_and`."""
    return all(eval_clause(part, params) for part in conjunction.split(" and "))


def eval_when(expr: str, params: dict[str, str]) -> bool:
    """A full predicate: conjunctions joined by ` or ` — any may hold. Empty ⇒ always (defensive;
    absent ⇒ "always" upstream, per `when_of`/DEFAULT_WHEN). Mirrors `eval_condition`."""
    text = expr.strip()
    if not text:
        return True
    return any(eval_and(part, params) for part in text.split(" or "))


def _relevant_keys(*exprs: str) -> set[str]:
    keys: set[str] = set()
    for expr in exprs:
        keys.update(key for key, _ in _WHEN_IN_RE.findall(expr))
        keys.update(key for key, _ in _WHEN_CMP_RE.findall(expr))
    return keys


def when_domains(rows: list[dict]) -> dict[str, set[str]]:
    """{parameter: observed value set} over EVERY row's `materializes-when`, plus the unset/empty
    string every key admits (a param absent from a scaffold's --params reads as "", matching
    `skill-union-assert.sh`'s `PARAM[$key]-` default). CORPUS-DERIVED, not a declared enum: sound
    whenever every legal value of every referenced parameter already appears somewhere in the
    registry's own predicates — true today by inspection (clarifications.md DEC-001), and a bounded,
    stated guarantee rather than a silent one if that ever stops holding.
    """
    domains: dict[str, set[str]] = {}
    for row in rows:
        when = normalize_when(row.get("materializes-when", DEFAULT_WHEN))
        for key, rhs in _WHEN_IN_RE.findall(when):
            values = domains.setdefault(key, {""})
            values.update(_unquote_when(item) for item in rhs.split(",") if _unquote_when(item))
        for key, rhs in _WHEN_CMP_RE.findall(when):
            domains.setdefault(key, {""}).add(_unquote_when(rhs))
    return domains


def _combinations(keys: set[str], domains: dict[str, set[str]]):
    if not keys:
        yield {}
        return
    ordered = sorted(keys)
    value_lists = [sorted(domains.get(key, {""})) for key in ordered]
    for combo in itertools.product(*value_lists):
        yield dict(zip(ordered, combo))


def when_implies(referencing_when: str, referenced_when: str, domains: dict[str, set[str]]):
    """(bool, witness). True iff every parameter combination that satisfies `referencing_when` also
    satisfies `referenced_when`, checked by enumeration over `domains` (see `when_domains`). On
    failure, `witness` is the first combination where the referencing predicate holds and the
    referenced one does not.
    """
    keys = _relevant_keys(referencing_when, referenced_when)
    for params in _combinations(keys, domains):
        if eval_when(referencing_when, params) and not eval_when(referenced_when, params):
            return False, params
    return True, None


def when_satisfiable(when: str, domains: dict[str, set[str]]) -> bool:
    """Whether ANY parameter combination makes `when` true. `"false"` (or any predicate equivalent
    to it) is not — and a row whose own predicate can never hold never materializes into any
    product tree, so nothing it references can ever go missing FOR IT (.github#2366 AC-003)."""
    keys = _relevant_keys(when)
    return any(eval_when(when, params) for params in _combinations(keys, domains))


def _skill_files(skill_dir: str) -> list[str]:
    """`SKILL.md` plus every file under `references/` — the same surface a materialized product
    tree actually delivers for this skill (the `source:` field alone names only `SKILL.md`)."""
    files = []
    skill_md = os.path.join(skill_dir, "SKILL.md")
    if os.path.isfile(skill_md):
        files.append(skill_md)
    references_dir = os.path.join(skill_dir, "references")
    if os.path.isdir(references_dir):
        for root, _dirs, names in sorted(os.walk(references_dir)):
            for name in sorted(names):
                files.append(os.path.join(root, name))
    return files


def cross_references(rows: list[dict], repos_root: str) -> list[dict]:
    """Check 7. See the module docstring and this section's header comment."""
    findings: list[dict] = []
    domains = when_domains(rows)
    by_id = {row["id"]: row for row in rows}

    for row in rows:
        skill_id = row["id"]
        referencing_when = normalize_when(row.get("materializes-when", DEFAULT_WHEN))
        if not when_satisfiable(referencing_when, domains):
            continue

        source = row.get("source")
        if not isinstance(source, str):
            continue
        skill_dir = os.path.dirname(os.path.join(repos_root, source))
        if not os.path.isdir(skill_dir):
            continue  # a missing/unreadable source is already `source-exists`'s finding to make

        referenced_ids: set[str] = set()
        for path in _skill_files(skill_dir):
            raw = read_bytes(path)
            if raw is None:
                continue
            text = raw.decode("utf-8", errors="replace")
            referenced_ids.update(_SIBLING_PATH_RE.findall(text))

        for referenced_id in sorted(referenced_ids - {skill_id}):
            target = by_id.get(referenced_id)
            if target is None:
                findings.append(
                    {
                        "id": skill_id,
                        "check": "cross-references",
                        "declared": referenced_id,
                        "detail": (
                            f"references `.../skills/{referenced_id}/…` but no registry row "
                            f"declares id {referenced_id!r} — the reference cannot be guaranteed "
                            "to resolve in any tree that materializes this skill"
                        ),
                    }
                )
                continue

            referenced_when = normalize_when(target.get("materializes-when", DEFAULT_WHEN))
            implied, witness = when_implies(referencing_when, referenced_when, domains)
            if implied:
                continue
            findings.append(
                {
                    "id": skill_id,
                    "check": "cross-references",
                    "declared": referenced_id,
                    "detail": (
                        f"references `.../skills/{referenced_id}/…` (materializes-when: "
                        f"`{referenced_when}`) but this skill's own materializes-when "
                        f"(`{referencing_when}`) does not imply it — e.g. under {witness!r} this "
                        f"skill would materialize and {referenced_id!r} would not"
                    ),
                }
            )

    return findings


def _load_registry(registry_path: str) -> dict:
    try:
        import yaml
    except ImportError:  # pragma: no cover - environment guard
        sys.exit("fsgg-skill-registry-check: PyYAML is required (python -m pip install pyyaml)")
    with open(registry_path) as handle:
        return yaml.safe_load(handle)


def check(registry_path: str, repos_root: str) -> list[dict]:
    registry = _load_registry(registry_path)

    findings: list[dict] = []
    declarers, manifest_findings = manifest_index(repos_root, registry["skills"])

    for row in registry["skills"]:
        skill_id, source = row["id"], row["source"]
        # An unquoted all-digit digest parses as an int, and a truncated one is silently wrong.
        # Both are registry-shape defects, so report rather than crash on them.
        declared = str(row["sha256"])
        if not DIGEST_RE.fullmatch(declared):
            findings.append(
                {
                    "id": skill_id,
                    "check": "digest-shape",
                    "detail": f"sha256 is not 64 lowercase hex chars: {declared!r} (quote it in YAML?)",
                }
            )
            continue

        source_path = os.path.join(repos_root, source)

        raw = read_bytes(source_path)
        if raw is None:
            findings.append(
                {
                    "id": skill_id,
                    "check": "source-exists",
                    "detail": f"source not found: {source}",
                }
            )
            continue

        actual = canonical_digest(raw)
        if actual != declared:
            findings.append(
                {
                    "id": skill_id,
                    "check": "digest-matches",
                    "detail": f"declared {declared[:8]}… but source hashes to {actual[:8]}…",
                    "declared": declared,
                    "actual": actual,
                }
            )

    findings.extend(predicates(registry["skills"], declarers))
    findings.extend(manifest_findings)
    findings.extend(completeness(registry["skills"], declarers))
    findings.extend(roster_reachable(registry_path, repos_root))
    findings.extend(parameter_vocabulary(registry))
    findings.extend(cross_references(registry["skills"], repos_root))
    return findings


# The separating commas live in the PADDED fields, not the template — a comma in both yields
# `id: x,   ,` and a YAML parse error, which is how the first draft of this failed.
#
# `materializes-when` stays last because `reconcile_predicates` anchors on that field order.
ROW_TEMPLATE = "  - {{ id: {id}scope: {scope}owner: {owner}source: {source} sha256: {sha256}, materializes-when: {when} }}"


def format_when(when: str) -> str:
    """A `materializes-when` as YAML: `always` is a bare token, every real predicate is quoted.

    json.dumps, not f'"{when}"' — a YAML double-quoted scalar escapes exactly as JSON does, so a
    predicate containing a `"` round-trips instead of terminating the scalar early.
    """
    return when if when == DEFAULT_WHEN else json.dumps(when)


def format_row(row: dict) -> str:
    """One hand-aligned YAML flow map, in the shape the surrounding rows are written in."""
    return ROW_TEMPLATE.format(
        id=f"{row['id']},".ljust(24),
        scope=f"{row['scope']},".ljust(9),
        owner=f"{row['owner']},".ljust(17),
        source=f"{row['source']},",
        sha256=row["sha256"],
        when=format_when(row["materializes-when"]),
    )


def _parse_row_line(line: str) -> dict | None:
    """The `- { id: ... }` flow map on a row line as a dict, or None if the line is not a row.

    A homed row is placed beside the siblings it belongs with (`_home_index`), which means reading
    the `owner:`/`materializes-when:` off the rows already in the file. Parsing the single flow map
    with PyYAML — the same parser `_load_registry` uses, always available on the `--write` path —
    is exact where a regex would have to re-implement the grammar.
    """
    stripped = line.lstrip()
    if not stripped.startswith("- {"):
        return None
    import yaml

    try:
        parsed = yaml.safe_load(stripped[2:])  # drop the "- " list marker; the rest is a flow map
    except yaml.YAMLError:
        return None
    return parsed if isinstance(parsed, dict) else None


def _home_index(lines: list[str], owner: str, when: object) -> int | None:
    """0-based index of the LAST row line sharing (owner, normalized `materializes-when`), or None.

    This is the placement a human made by hand in .github#1194: fs-gg-symbol-design after the last
    `fs-gg-rendering` row with `profile in [game, sample-pack]`, fs-gg-playtest after the last
    `fs-gg-game` row with the same predicate. Keying on (owner, predicate) reproduces exactly that,
    and a row whose siblings are not yet in the file returns None so the caller tail-appends rather
    than GUESSING a block.
    """
    target = normalize_when(when)
    found: int | None = None
    for i, line in enumerate(lines):
        row = _parse_row_line(line)
        if row is None:
            continue
        if row.get("owner") == owner and normalize_when(row.get("materializes-when", DEFAULT_WHEN)) == target:
            found = i
    return found


def _home_annotation(row: dict) -> list[str]:
    """The generated comment block written above a homed row (.github#299/#1200).

    Honest and minimal: it records that the POSITION and this comment are the tool's while the row's
    bytes are the manifest's — it does NOT fabricate the cross-repo reasoning a human annotation
    carries (which producer PR or ownership call). That is a human's to add in place later; the
    row is homed either way.
    """
    return [
        f"  # {row['id']} — homed beside its owner+predicate siblings and reconciled verbatim from\n",
        f"  # {row['owner']}'s producer manifest by `--write` (.github#299/.github#1200). The position\n",
        f"  # and this annotation are generated; the row's bytes are the manifest's.\n",
    ]


def place_rows(registry_path: str, findings: list[dict]) -> tuple[int, int]:
    """Home each addable row beside its siblings; tail-append the ones with none. Returns (homed, appended).

    Homing is the housekeeping `--write` used to punt to the operator: a row whose (owner, predicate)
    siblings are already in the file is inserted among them, so it never sits in the tail waiting for
    a hand re-home (the .github#1198 toil this closes). A row with no sibling is still tail-appended
    under the existing note — the tool homes what it can prove, and guesses nothing.
    """
    rows = sorted(
        (f["row"] for f in findings if f["check"] == "declared-completeness" and "row" in f),
        key=lambda r: r["id"],
    )
    if not rows:
        return 0, 0

    homed = 0
    unhomed: list[dict] = []
    for row in rows:
        # Re-read per row: an earlier homed row can be the sibling the next one homes beside, and it
        # keeps line bookkeeping trivial. The set added per run is a handful of rows, never a loop hot
        # path.
        with open(registry_path) as handle:
            lines = handle.read().splitlines(keepends=True)
        anchor = _home_index(lines, row["owner"], row.get("materializes-when", DEFAULT_WHEN))
        if anchor is None:
            unhomed.append(row)
            continue
        if not lines[anchor].endswith("\n"):
            lines[anchor] += "\n"
        lines[anchor + 1 : anchor + 1] = _home_annotation(row) + [format_row(row) + "\n"]
        with open(registry_path, "w") as handle:
            handle.write("".join(lines))
        homed += 1

    return homed, append_rows(registry_path, unhomed)


def append_rows(registry_path: str, rows: list[dict]) -> int:
    """Append each row that could not be homed, after the last row, under the re-home note."""
    if not rows:
        return 0

    with open(registry_path) as handle:
        lines = handle.read().splitlines(keepends=True)

    # Anchor on the LAST row rather than EOF: a trailing comment must stay trailing.
    last = max((i for i, line in enumerate(lines) if line.startswith("  - { id:")), default=None)
    if last is None:
        sys.exit(f"fsgg-skill-registry-check: no `- {{ id: ... }}` rows found in {registry_path}")
    if not lines[last].endswith("\n"):
        lines[last] += "\n"

    block = [
        "\n",
        "  # APPENDED by `fsgg-skill-registry-check --write` from the producer manifests (.github#289).\n",
        "  # Reconciled, not hand-authored: scope/sha256/materializes-when verbatim from the manifest\n",
        "  # entry, `source` from its `supplied-by`. Re-home and annotate these into the block they\n",
        "  # belong to, then set `updated:` and prepend a registry/skills.CHANGELOG.md entry.\n",
    ]
    block += [format_row(row) + "\n" for row in sorted(rows, key=lambda r: r["id"])]

    lines[last + 1 : last + 1] = block
    with open(registry_path, "w") as handle:
        handle.write("".join(lines))
    return len(rows)


# `updated:` is a top-level scalar; anchored at line start so nothing inside a row's flow map matches.
UPDATED_RE = re.compile(r'^(updated:[ \t]*)"[^"]*"', re.MULTILINE)


def bump_updated(registry_path: str, date: str) -> bool:
    """Set the registry's `updated:` scalar to DATE. Returns whether it changed (.github#299/#1200)."""
    with open(registry_path) as handle:
        text = handle.read()
    new_text, count = UPDATED_RE.subn(rf'\g<1>"{date}"', text, count=1)
    if not count or new_text == text:
        return False
    with open(registry_path, "w") as handle:
        handle.write(new_text)
    return True


def prepend_changelog(changelog_path: str, date: str, summary: str) -> bool:
    """Prepend one dated entry at the top of the changelog's Entries list (newest first).

    Inserts AFTER the `## Entries` header and its `<!-- Prepend new entries here … -->` guidance
    block, before the newest real entry — so the `- **YYYY-MM-DD** — HEADER` EXAMPLE inside that
    comment is never mistaken for the insertion point. A changelog missing the header is left
    untouched and reported as unchanged rather than guessed at.
    """
    try:
        with open(changelog_path) as handle:
            text = handle.read()
    except OSError:
        return False

    marker = text.find("## Entries")
    if marker == -1:
        return False
    # Skip past the guidance comment so its example bullet is not the anchor.
    comment_close = text.find("-->", marker)
    search_from = comment_close + len("-->") if comment_close != -1 else marker
    first_bullet = text.find("\n- **", search_from)
    if first_bullet == -1:
        return False
    insert_at = first_bullet + 1  # start of the newest existing entry line
    entry = f"- **{date}** — {summary}\n\n"
    with open(changelog_path, "w") as handle:
        handle.write(text[:insert_at] + entry + text[insert_at:])
    return True


def reconcile(registry_path: str, findings: list[dict]) -> int:
    """Rewrite only the sha256 of each digest-mismatched row, preserving the hand-aligned YAML."""
    stale = {f["id"]: f["actual"] for f in findings if f["check"] == "digest-matches"}
    if not stale:
        return 0

    with open(registry_path) as handle:
        text = handle.read()

    written = 0
    for skill_id, actual in stale.items():
        # Anchor on the row's own `id:` so we can never rewrite a neighbour's digest.
        pattern = re.compile(
            rf"(\{{\s*id:\s*{re.escape(skill_id)}\s*,[^}}]*?sha256:\s*)([0-9a-f]{{64}})"
        )
        text, count = pattern.subn(rf"\g<1>{actual}", text, count=1)
        if count != 1:
            sys.exit(f"fsgg-skill-registry-check: could not locate the sha256 of row {skill_id}")
        written += 1

    with open(registry_path, "w") as handle:
        handle.write(text)
    return written


def reconcile_predicates(registry_path: str, findings: list[dict]) -> int:
    """Rewrite only the `materializes-when` of each diverged row, preserving the hand-aligned YAML.

    A row whose declarers contradict each other carries no `actual` and is skipped — `--write`'s
    re-check then leaves it as the finding it is, rather than picking a producer to believe.
    """
    stale = {f["id"]: f["actual"] for f in findings if f["check"] == "predicate-matches" and "actual" in f}
    if not stale:
        return 0

    with open(registry_path) as handle:
        text = handle.read()

    written = 0
    for skill_id, actual in stale.items():
        # Anchor on the row's own `id:`, as reconcile() does. `[^}]` cannot cross the row's closing
        # brace, so this can never rewrite a neighbour's predicate; and the `\s*,` after the id keeps
        # `fs-gg-scene` from matching a `fs-gg-scene-extra` row.
        # ASSUMES, as every row in this file holds: `materializes-when` is the row's LAST field, and
        # no predicate contains a `}`. Both are true of the ADR-0017 grammar (bare tokens and
        # bracketed lists); a predicate carrying `}` would slice the value group early, so a future
        # grammar that admits one must anchor this on the field order instead.
        pattern = re.compile(
            rf"(\{{\s*id:\s*{re.escape(skill_id)}\s*,[^}}]*?materializes-when:\s*)([^}}]*?)(\s*\}})"
        )
        # A function replacement, not a template: `format_when` emits a JSON scalar, whose
        # backslashes would otherwise be read as group references.
        text, count = pattern.subn(
            lambda m, value=actual: f"{m.group(1)}{format_when(value)}{m.group(3)}", text, count=1
        )
        if count != 1:
            sys.exit(f"fsgg-skill-registry-check: could not locate the materializes-when of row {skill_id}")
        written += 1

    with open(registry_path, "w") as handle:
        handle.write(text)
    return written


def main() -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(add_help=True, description=__doc__.splitlines()[0])
    parser.add_argument("--registry", default="registry/skills.yml")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--repos-root",
        default=".repos",
        help="directory holding the producer checkouts (FS.GG.SDD/, FS.GG.Rendering/, FS.GG.Game/)",
    )
    parser.add_argument("--json", action="store_true", help="emit findings as JSON")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--write", action="store_true", help="reconcile stale sha256 / materializes-when in place"
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--now",
        metavar="DATE",
        help="today's date (YYYY-MM-DD). With --write, bump `updated:` to DATE and prepend a dated "
        "registry/skills.CHANGELOG.md entry naming the reconcile — the housekeeping the protocol used "
        "to leave to the operator (.github#299/#1200). INJECTED, never read from a clock, so the "
        "reconcile stays deterministic. Without it those two steps are skipped and the reminder printed.",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--changelog",
        default="registry/skills.CHANGELOG.md",
        help="the changelog --write --now prepends its reconcile entry to",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--normalize-when",
        metavar="PREDICATE",
        help="reference seam: print normalize_when(PREDICATE) and exit — the canonical comparable "
        "form the drift check (check 6) uses. Lets the cross-impl conformance fixture pin this "
        "normalizer against the shell evaluator and the typed Fsgg.Registry validator so the three "
        "cannot silently drift (.github#398). Pure-stdlib: needs no registry, --repos-root, or PyYAML.",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--digest",
        metavar="PATH",
        help="reference seam: print canonical_digest(PATH's bytes) and exit — the canonical body "
        "digest this checker compares against. The counterpart of `skill-union-assert.sh --digest`, "
        "and the seam tests/skill-union/skillmirror-conformance.sh drives so all THREE "
        "implementations of one digest (this, the shell, and Fsgg.SkillMirror.sha256) are pinned "
        "over one shared table rather than pairwise (.github#1547). Pure-stdlib: needs no registry, "
        "--repos-root, or PyYAML.",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--producers",
        action="store_true",
        help="print, one per line, the FS-GG checkout names registry/repos.yml rosters — the set "
        "`--repos-root` must hold — and exit. THE CLONE LOOPS IN CI READ THIS RATHER THAN LISTING "
        "REPOS THEMSELVES (.github#2547): a hardcoded loop excluded FS.GG.Templates for as long as "
        "it had been a skill producer, and the `roster_reachable` arm that now reds on an absent "
        "one would merely have moved the same list one file over if the loop kept its own copy. "
        "Pure-stdlib + PyYAML: needs no --repos-root.",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--baseline-registry",
        metavar="PATH",
        help="the registry as it stands on the MERGE BASE. Turns the verdict from 'is the world "
        "coherent?' into 'did THIS CHANGE break it?' — a finding that already existed on the base is "
        "reported but does not fail. For PRs only; `main` and the schedule stay absolute (.github#422).",
    )
    args = parser.parse_args()

    if args.normalize_when is not None:
        print(normalize_when(args.normalize_when))
        return 0

    if args.digest is not None:
        # An UNREADABLE file is exit 2, never a printed digest: this seam is the reference a
        # conformance table is measured against, and the one thing it must never do is answer
        # confidently about bytes it did not read (#266). `read_bytes` returns None on OSError.
        raw = read_bytes(args.digest)
        if raw is None:
            sys.exit(f"fsgg-skill-registry-check: --digest '{args.digest}' is not a readable file.")
        print(canonical_digest(raw))
        return 0

    if args.producers:
        # An UNREADABLE roster is exit 2, never an empty list: a clone loop reading this would
        # otherwise clone NOTHING and every arm below would run green over an empty world — the
        # fail-open shape (#266) this seam exists to close.
        names, error = roster(args.registry)
        if error:
            sys.exit(f"fsgg-skill-registry-check: --producers: {error}")
        print("\n".join(names))
        return 0

    if not os.path.isdir(args.repos_root):
        sys.exit(
            f"fsgg-skill-registry-check: --repos-root '{args.repos_root}' is not a directory. "
            "Check out the producer repos there (see .github/workflows/skill-registry-coherence.yml)."
        )

    # AN IGNORED ARGUMENT IS INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM AN HONOURED ONE. `--write` returns below without
    # ever reaching the baseline logic, so the two together would accept the flag, consume it, and
    # silently do nothing with it — and the caller would be told the write was judged against the base
    # when it was not. The org already enforces this rule in `repos.sh` (`need_val`) and in the engine's
    # own parser; a reconcile is not a verdict, so refuse the combination rather than ignoring half of it.
    if args.write and args.baseline_registry:
        sys.exit(
            "fsgg-skill-registry-check: --write and --baseline-registry are mutually exclusive. "
            "--write RECONCILES the registry; --baseline-registry JUDGES a change against its merge "
            "base. Honouring one would mean silently ignoring the other."
        )

    # --now stamps a --write; on its own it would be an accepted-but-ignored argument, the very shape
    # the --write/--baseline guard above refuses. Reject it rather than silently doing nothing with it.
    if args.now is not None:
        if not args.write:
            sys.exit(
                "fsgg-skill-registry-check: --now only applies with --write (it stamps a reconcile's "
                "`updated:` and changelog). Pass --write, or drop --now."
            )
        if not re.fullmatch(r"\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}", args.now):
            sys.exit(
                f"fsgg-skill-registry-check: --now '{args.now}' is not an ISO date (YYYY-MM-DD)."
            )

    findings = check(args.registry, args.repos_root)

    if args.write:
        homed, appended = place_rows(args.registry, findings)
        written = reconcile(args.registry, findings)
        repredicated = reconcile_predicates(args.registry, findings)
        summary = (
            f"reconciled {written} sha256 row(s) and {repredicated} materializes-when value(s); "
            f"homed {homed} and appended {appended} row(s)"
        )
        # The housekeeping the operator used to run by hand (.github#299/#1200), only when --write
        # actually changed the file AND a date was injected — never stamp a no-op. Cosmetic to the
        # coherence verdict, so it may run before the re-check below.
        bumped = logged = False
        if args.now and (homed or appended or written or repredicated):
            bumped = bump_updated(args.registry, args.now)
            logged = prepend_changelog(
                args.changelog,
                args.now,
                f"RECONCILE (auto; .github#299/#1200): `fsgg-skill-registry-check --write` {summary} "
                "from the producer manifests. registry = manifest = bytes.",
            )

        print(f"skill-registry: {summary} in {args.registry}")

        # Re-check from disk. --write cannot invent a missing source, add a row the manifest gave no
        # `supplied-by` for, or pick a side when two producers declare contradictory predicates — and an added row
        # whose manifest digest disagrees with the body surfaces here rather than on someone else's CI.
        blocked = check(args.registry, args.repos_root)
        if blocked:
            for finding in blocked:
                print(f"  still unresolved [{finding['check']}] {finding['id']}: {finding['detail']}")
            return 1
        if not args.now:
            print("  remember: set `updated:` and prepend a registry/skills.CHANGELOG.md entry")
        elif bumped or logged:
            did = []
            if bumped:
                did.append(f"bumped `updated:` to {args.now}")
            if logged:
                did.append(f"prepended a {args.changelog} entry")
            print("  " + " and ".join(did))
        else:
            print("  nothing to reconcile — `updated:` and the changelog left unchanged")
        return 0

    # ---- the PR verdict: "did THIS CHANGE break it?", not "is the world perfect?" (#422) ----------
    #
    # THE JAM THIS UNDOES. `check` had ONE verdict — any finding, exit 1 — and the findings fall into
    # two classes that `--write` itself already distinguishes: MECHANICAL ones it reconciles (a stale
    # sha256, a diverged predicate, a missing row) and JUDGEMENT ones it deliberately refuses (a
    # vanished source; two producers contradicting each other about who is canonical, #295).
    #
    # With one all-or-nothing verdict, that standing red sat on the autofix bot's own PR. The bot ran
    # daily, reconciled the stale digests, opened its PR — and the PR's `registry-coherence` check
    # failed on the judgement findings the bot had correctly refused to touch. So the PR never merged,
    # the mechanical drift never landed, and it ACCUMULATED behind a red nobody could clear: eight
    # stale digests on `main` at the time of writing, and #595 open and permanently red. A daily bot
    # that runs, succeeds, and achieves nothing — which is the ornament this org keeps building
    # (epic #416), inside the gate that is supposed to catch exactly that.
    #
    # So a PR is judged on what it DID. A finding already present on the merge base is not this
    # change's doing and does not fail it — it is printed, loudly, and `main` stays RED on it, because
    # `main`/`schedule` never pass a baseline and their verdict is unchanged. Nothing is hidden and no
    # standing problem gets quieter; a correct change simply stops being hostage to it.
    # A FINDING'S IDENTITY INCLUDES WHAT THE REGISTRY *SAID*, not just which row it was about.
    #
    # Keyed on (check, id) alone, a PR could rewrite an ALREADY-stale digest to a DIFFERENT wrong value
    # and still be waved through as "inherited" — the row was broken before and is broken now, so the
    # key matches. But that PR made a real, wrong edit to `skills.yml`, and this gate is the thing that
    # is supposed to catch it. The declared value is therefore part of the identity: change it and the
    # finding is NEW, whatever it was before.
    def key(f: dict) -> tuple[str, str, str]:
        return (f["check"], f["id"], str(f.get("declared", "")))

    baseline_keys: set[tuple[str, str, str]] | None = None
    if args.baseline_registry:
        if not os.path.isfile(args.baseline_registry):
            # NOT an empty baseline. A baseline we could not read would mark every finding NEW, which
            # fails closed and is therefore "safe" — but it would fail every PR for a reason that has
            # nothing to do with the PR, and the operator would learn to ignore this check. Say it.
            sys.exit(
                f"fsgg-skill-registry-check: --baseline-registry '{args.baseline_registry}' is not a "
                "file. Refusing to guess what the merge base declared."
            )

        # A BLINDED READ VOIDS THE COMPARISON. It does not get inherited (#425).
        #
        # `manifest-found` means a producer manifest could not be read — so the rows from that producer
        # were never examined on EITHER side. And because that finding depends only on the producer
        # bytes, which both sides share, it fires identically on base and HEAD and would ALWAYS be
        # classed "inherited" and forgiven. Every PR would then print "introduced NO new incoherence"
        # while the gate was blind to an entire producer's rows — a claim with no evidence behind it,
        # which is the exact fail-open (#266, #425) this gate exists to prevent, rebuilt inside it.
        #
        # So: if we could not look, we do not get to say the change was clean. Fall back to the
        # absolute verdict and name the cause.
        blinded = [f for f in findings if f["check"] in BLINDING_CHECKS]
        if blinded:
            print(
                f"skill-registry: the check was BLINDED on {len(blinded)} producer(s) — "
                "the baseline comparison is void:"
            )
            for finding in blinded:
                print(f"  [{finding['check']}] {finding['id']}: {finding['detail']}")
            print(
                "  A manifest that could not be READ is not a finding that can be inherited: the rows\n"
                "  from that producer were never checked on EITHER side, so 'this change introduced\n"
                "  nothing' would be a claim with no evidence behind it. Judging absolutely instead.\n"
            )
        else:
            # The SAME producer bytes for both sides. The baseline differs from HEAD in exactly one
            # thing — the registry — so a producer that moved between two clones cannot masquerade as a
            # finding this PR introduced.
            baseline_keys = {key(f) for f in check(args.baseline_registry, args.repos_root)}

    def is_new(f: dict) -> bool:
        return baseline_keys is None or key(f) not in baseline_keys

    introduced = [f for f in findings if is_new(f)]
    inherited = [f for f in findings if not is_new(f)]

    if args.json:
        print(
            json.dumps(
                {
                    "findings": findings,
                    "introduced": introduced,
                    "inherited": inherited,
                },
                indent=2,
                sort_keys=True,
            )
        )
        return 1 if introduced else 0

    if inherited:
        print(
            f"skill-registry: {len(inherited)} PRE-EXISTING finding(s) — already broken on the merge "
            "base, NOT introduced here:"
        )
        for finding in inherited:
            print(f"  [{finding['check']}] {finding['id']}: {finding['detail']}")
        print(
            "  These do NOT fail this PR — you did not break them and, for a vanished source, you\n"
            "  cannot fix them mechanically. They are still real: `main`'s own\n"
            "  run of this gate is RED on them and stays red until somebody lands the required fix.\n"
        )

    if introduced:
        verb = "introduced by this change" if baseline_keys is not None else "— registry ≠ manifest ≠ bytes"
        print(f"skill-registry: {len(introduced)} finding(s) {verb}")
        for finding in introduced:
            print(f"  [{finding['check']}] {finding['id']}: {finding['detail']}")
        print(
            "\nskills.yml is reconciled FROM the producer manifests (ADR-0017), never hand-authored.\n"
            "Fix: scripts/fsgg-skill-registry-check --write --repos-root <dir>, then set `updated:`\n"
            "and prepend a dated registry/skills.CHANGELOG.md entry naming the producer PR."
        )
    elif not inherited:
        print("skill-registry: coherent — registry = manifest = bytes")
    else:
        print("skill-registry: this change introduced NO new incoherence.")

    return 1 if introduced else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
